<article class="env-lemma" id="08FG">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08FG">Lemma <span data-tag="08FG">21.17.15</span>.</a> Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed site. Let $\mathcal{F}$ be an $\mathcal{O}$-module. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$\mathcal{F}$ is a flat $\mathcal{O}$-module, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\text{Tor}_1^\mathcal {O}(\mathcal{F}, \mathcal{G}) = 0$ for every $\mathcal{O}$-module $\mathcal{G}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $\mathcal{F}$ is flat, then $\mathcal{F} \otimes _\mathcal {O} -$ is an exact functor and the satellites vanish. Conversely assume (2) holds. Then if $\mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{H}$ is injective with cokernel $\mathcal{Q}$, the long exact sequence of $\text{Tor}$ shows that the kernel of $\mathcal{F} \otimes _\mathcal {O} \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{F} \otimes _\mathcal {O} \mathcal{H}$ is a quotient of $\text{Tor}_1^\mathcal {O}(\mathcal{F}, \mathcal{Q})$ which is zero by assumption. Hence $\mathcal{F}$ is flat. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>